  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 1254 AM CDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nFriday night and Saturday, upper ridge over the Central CONUS \nbegins to break down and move eastward. Surface high pressure \nwill have shifted to the east of the area Friday night resulting\nin south to southwesterly low level flow into the region, we \nwill start to see temperatures and moisture gradually increase \ninto Saturday. Precipitation chances have been trending upwards\nfor Saturday, as a wave slowly approaches late Friday night and\nmeanders through the area on Saturday. Some guidance would \nindicate the potential for a decaying line of showers/storms in \nthe morning, with the potential for redevelopment along any \noutflow that afternoon. Although, much uncertainty exists owing \nto plenty of dry air for this to battle and a recent trend in \nthe models to push the terminus of the low level jet northward \ninto southeast Minnesota and Wisconsin. This will be something \nthat we keep an eye on over the coming days. Will stick with the\nNBM PoPs at this point, where it highlights areas largely north\nof Interstate 80 in 20-40% chances. \n\nSaturday night onward, ridging aloft shifts to the eastward \nwith better return flow into the area. There are some \ndifferences between the models in the strength and placement of \nthe ridge late this weekend into early next week. Model spread \non forecast high temperatures next week varies from 6 to 8 \ndegrees on Monday to as much as 10 degrees on Wednesday and \nThursday so there is some variability in forecasts with some \nmodels showing forecast high temperatures in the mid to upper \n90s. Decided to limit high temperatures during this period to \nthe upper 80s to lower 90s due to the model variability which is\nclose to the NBM 50th percentile forecast. With increasing \nhumidity as well, some areas may have heat indices approaching \n100 especially for area in southeast Iowa, west central Illinois\nand far northeast Missouri. \n\nThe strengthening Plains low level jet will allow PoPs to \ncontinue overnight Saturday into Sunday, largely north of \nInterstate 80. Upper level flow will generally be zonal to \nsouthwesterly through the remainder of the forecast period, \nwhich may put us under the active storm path. Thus, if this \npattern holds true, we could be heading into a potentially more \nactive stretch of weather into next week with daily chances for\nshowers and storms especially for areas north of a Fairfield to\nGalesburg line. Global models are in general agreement on \nchances for precipitation, with some differences in the timing \nand track of these shortwaves. Although, the NBM introduces \ndaily PoPs (20-40%) Sunday through Thursday. Given the\nuncertainties in the forecast it is too early to determine any \nsevere threat.",
